[#:seon.task{:name "a-branch-forked-before-an-adoption-cannot-record-its-tests"
             :kind :function
             :intent "After root restarted default onto 7ab559153 (which added `:seon.test.run/evaluator`), agent astra-context-cost's branch :agent-f1c3991d9467, forked before that adoption, recorded the error \"Bad entity attribute :seon.test.run/evaluator ... not defined in current schema\" from `seon.db/transact!` (signature 5955dae4f006, 2026-10-10T14:32Z): `seon.test/admit-run` writes the attribute the JVM's code now produces, but adoption (`seon.cluster/adopt-entities!`) installed it on the cluster branch only, so every agent branch older than an adoption cannot admit a test run until it is recreated. Done when a branch forked before an adoption that adds an attribute admits a test run that writes it, or the test request refuses once by name with the fix."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/adopt-entities! seon.test/admit-run seon.cluster.registry/fork!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "readers-of-the-latest-result-ignore-its-evaluator"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Since 7ab559153 `seon.test/select` reuses only runs of the requesting evaluator (`:seon.test.run/evaluator`), but the other readers of recorded evidence take the latest result whatever ran it: `seon.test.runner/latest-results` (read by `seon.context/proofs`, so `seon.context/task-done?`, and by `seon.turn` for `:seon.agent/reached-red`), `seon.test/recorded-result`, `seon.test/verified?` and `seon.cluster.branch-evidence/branch-evidence`. Filtering them to the agent's own evaluator is not simply right (lane interp-honest, 2026-10-11): a host-bound change is proven by `seon.test.platform/record!`, a compiled run recorded on the agent's branch, which `task-done?` and branch evidence must accept; and compiled is the reference semantics, so a compiled green is the stronger evidence for an interpreted agent, while an interpreted green read by root is the weaker. Options for root: (1) readers accept the agent's own evaluator and `:compiled` (an interpreted result never proves root's work; a compiled one proves anyone's); (2) strict: each reader accepts only its agent's evaluator, and check-platform completion reads compiled explicitly; (3) readers stay evaluator-agnostic and each result carries its evaluator so renders and messages show it. Recommended: (1). Done when the chosen rule is stated on each reader and a regression shows the excluded direction does not complete a proof."
             :must-read #{seon.test.runner/latest-results seon.test/verified? seon.context/task-done? seon.test/select}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-result-is-reused-only-by-the-evaluator-that-produced-it"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Astra day audit 2026-10-10, finding 3: `seon.test/select` reused a member's recorded result by branch, input digest and reach digest alone, so on one branch a non-root agent's interpreted request (`seon.test/resolve-test` with `:seon.test/interpreted-files?`) answered root's compiled green without running it (default, agent interp-honest-head homed on the cluster branch: executed 0, reused 1). Done when `seon.test/run-request` declares the request's evaluator (`:seon.test.run/evaluator`, compiled host or interpreted SCI), `seon.test/admit-run` records it on the run and in its admission scope, and `seon.test/select` reuses and continues only runs of that same evaluator."
             :must-read #{seon.test/select seon.test/admit-run seon.test/run-request seon.test/resolve-test seon.test.platform/record!}}
  #:seon.task{:name "an-unchanged-red-is-reused-after-the-agent-redefines-what-it-reaches"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Lane merge-gate-unloaded, 2026-10-10, check runs add7cdb4a028 and c90e19d61a4d: after an agent redefined `tq.land/area`, `my.test/check` reused a red. The reuse key was sound: the red was recorded at the redefinition's own reach digest, by a run whose world called the old body (lane stale-reuse reproduced it with one own-branch agent defining `area` and `area-test`, then redefining `area`). `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` carried the context's own Var of an agent test (analyzed against the context's `area` Var) over the regenerated program, which replaced `area` with a Var of its own; the carried test called the old root, and `seon.test/clean-request-handle` forks that context for every member. Done when the program's interpreted tests are never carried as the context's own, so the redefinition's run is green and an unchanged check reuses it."
